# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
mill.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: mill.logic has "desired → proof"; mill.logic.2 and mill.logic.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "each person, so far as he believes it to be attainable, desires his own happiness" is true, which the second allows
mill.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps mill.logic, to: Suppose "each person, so far as he believes it to be attainable, desires his own happiness" is true and "we have all the proof which the case admits of that happiness is a good" is false. Is S3, "In like manner, I apprehend, the sole evidence it is possible to produce that anything is desirable, is that people do actually desire it.", true then?
mill.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: mill.logic has nothing from it; mill.logic.2 and mill.logic.3 have "desired → proof"; they differ when only "each person, so far as he believes it to be attainable, desires his own happiness" is true, which the first allows
mill.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps mill.logic.2 and mill.logic.3, to: Suppose "each person, so far as he believes it to be attainable, desires his own happiness" is true and "we have all the proof which the case admits of that happiness is a good" is false. Is S5, "No reason can be given why the general happiness is desirable, except that each person, so far as he believes it to be attainable, desires his own happiness.", true then?
mill.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 3 ways: mill.logic has "desired" and "own_good → general_good"; mill.logic.2 has "desired" and "proof → own_good"; they differ when only "each person, so far as he believes it to be attainable, desires his own happiness" and "we have all the proof which the case admits of that happiness is a good" are true, which the first allows; mill.logic.3 has "desired" and "proof → own_good" and "own_good → general_good"
mill.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered no, which keeps mill.logic.2 and mill.logic.3, to: Suppose "each person, so far as he believes it to be attainable, desires his own happiness" and "we have all the proof which the case admits of that happiness is a good" are true and "each person's happiness is a good to that person" and "the general happiness is a good to the aggregate of all persons" are false. Is S6, "This, however, being a fact, we have not only all the proof which the case admits of, but all which it is possible to require, that happiness is a good: that each person's happiness is a good to that person, and the general happiness, therefore, a good to the aggregate of all persons.", true then?
mill.logic: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: mill.logic has "proof → own_good" and "general_good → end_of_conduct"; mill.logic.2 has "own_good → general_good" and "own_good ∧ general_good → end_of_conduct"; they differ when only "we have all the proof which the case admits of that happiness is a good" and "each person's happiness is a good to that person" are true, which the first allows; mill.logic.3 has "general_good → end_of_conduct"
mill.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: mill.logic and mill.logic.3 have "end_of_conduct ∧ criterion"; mill.logic.2 has "criterion"; they differ when only "happiness is one of the criteria of morality" is true, which the second allows
mill.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps mill.logic and mill.logic.3, to: Suppose "happiness is one of the criteria of morality" is true and "happiness has made out its title as one of the ends of conduct" is false. Is the conclusion, "Happiness has made out its title as one of the ends of conduct, and consequently one of the criteria of morality.", true then?
mill.md:1: argue/unfaithful: mill.logic P1 (S3): the sentence has no word of "we have all the proof which the case admits of that happiness is a good": is it the sentence P1 came from?
mill.md:1: argue/negation: mill.logic.2 P1 (S5): the sentence says "no", but P1 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if each person, so far as he believes it to be attainable, desires his own happiness, then we have all the proof which the case admits of that happiness is a good"
mill.md:1: argue/negation: mill.logic.3 P1 (S5): the sentence says "no", but P1 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if each person, so far as he believes it to be attainable, desires his own happiness, then we have all the proof which the case admits of that happiness is a good"
